+# MLFF adaptive training stop specification
+
+Status: implemented in mdstats 0.20.124a0 (`ADAPT-STOP1`)
+
+## Scope
+
+ADAPT-STOP1 turns the fixed common ADAPT-MON1 force-RMSE monitors into a criterion-driven
+training boundary. It changes epoch termination only. It does not rank epochs, choose a run
+champion, retire EVAL-MF, or change final verification policy; those remain later gates.
+
+The selected learned-model precision from ADAPT-PREC1 is preserved during all monitor inference:
+`single` executes the learned model in FP32 and `double` executes it in FP64. mdstats-owned metric
+accumulation and policy arithmetic remain FP64.
+
+## Normative policy
+
+Let the default full target force-RMSE boundary be
+
+$$
+T_{\max}=0.030\ \mathrm{eV/\AA}=30\ \mathrm{meV/\AA}.
+$$
+
+For positive target/replay score weights `w_T` and `w_R`, replay-enabled campaigns derive
+
+$$
+R_{\max}=\frac{w_T}{w_R}T_{\max}.
+$$
+
+The generated default is `w_T:w_R = 1:1`, hence `R_max = 30 meV/A`. A no-replay training mode
+omits replay eligibility and replay stopping entirely.
+
+One completed epoch is lightweight-candidate-eligible only if
+
+$$
+T_{mon}\le T_{\max}\quad\mathrm{and}\quad R_{mon}\le R_{\max}.
+$$
+
+Training stops cleanly after a durable epoch checkpoint when the first applicable terminal
+condition is reached:
+
+$$
+T_{mon}\le 0.80 T_{\max},
+$$
+
+$$
+R_{mon}\ge 1.20 R_{\max},
+$$
+
+or the hard `max_num_epochs` ceiling is reached. At the default 1:1 weighting the resulting
+geometry is:
+
+| Boundary | Default |
+|---|---:|
+| target candidate | 30 meV/A |
+| target-success stop | 24 meV/A |
+| replay candidate | 30 meV/A |
+| replay-exhaustion stop | 36 meV/A |
+| hard epoch ceiling | 30 epochs |
+
+If target success and replay exhaustion become true on the same epoch, the terminal reason is
+`target_success_and_replay_exhaustion`. The stop epoch itself is not automatically the selected
+checkpoint.
+
+## Foundation replay feasibility
+
+Before epoch 0, MACE's initial validation on the fixed 512-configuration true-label replay
+monitor is frozen as the foundation replay baseline. If the derived `R_max` is below that
+baseline, training fails closed because the configured weighting is asking the fine-tuned model
+to outperform its foundation on replay. The user may deliberately opt into that scientific
+request with `allow_replay_threshold_below_foundation_baseline = true`.
+
+The foundation baseline is immutable across `--restart_latest`. A resumed model's new
+`epoch=None` validation block must never replace it.
+
+## Runtime integration
+
+ADAPT-STOP1 launches no additional model inference. MACE 0.3.16 already evaluates the target
+and replay validation loaders at every configured evaluation interval. New production protocols
+require `eval_interval = 1` and `save_all_checkpoints = true`.
+
+mdstats installs a source-qualified in-process MACE training-loop patch. The order for every epoch
+is:
+
+1. train the epoch;
+2. evaluate the common target and true-label replay monitors;
+3. append MACE's validation JSONL rows;
+4. save the epoch checkpoint durably;
+5. record ADAPT-STOP1 epoch evidence;
+6. if terminal, break the MACE epoch loop cleanly;
+7. allow MACE's normal final-model publication path to execute.
+
+The parent process therefore does not kill MACE to implement a scientific stop, and an adaptive
+stop is not charged as a failed retry.
+
+## Durable evidence and exact restart
+
+Each adaptive run owns `adaptive_training_stop.json`. It is content-addressed and binds the
+adaptive policy digest. It records:
+
+- foundation true-replay RMSE and threshold-feasibility state;
+- every completed epoch's target and replay force RMSE;
+- candidate eligibility for each epoch;
+- terminal epoch and terminal reason; and
+- whether any lightweight-admissible checkpoint exists.
+
+Existing MACE checkpoints require matching adaptive-stop state before production restart. Missing,
+invalid, or policy-mismatched state fails closed. Re-recording an already durable epoch is
+idempotent only if the monitor values are identical; changed values are rejected.
+
+If the parent is interrupted after terminal stop evidence is durable but before MACE publishes its
+final model, `--restart_latest` revalidates the inputs, observes the terminal state, skips the epoch
+loop, and runs only normal MACE finalization. No extra training epoch is permitted.
+
+## Run outcomes
+
+A terminal adaptive state has one of two selection-readiness outcomes:
+
+- `admissible_checkpoint_available` — at least one completed epoch satisfied both lightweight
+  candidate boundaries;
+- `no_lightweight_admissible_checkpoint` — no completed epoch satisfied the candidate region.
+
+ADAPT-STOP1 deliberately does not select among admissible epochs. ADAPT-RANK1 will later compute
+the weight-coupled lightweight score and freeze exactly one champion per independent run.
+EVAL-MF remains the production evaluator in 0.20.124a0 until ADAPT-EVAL1 is implemented.
+
+## Generated configuration
+
+New campaigns expose the policy as:
+
+```toml
+[training]
+max_num_epochs = 30
+target_stop_fraction = 0.80
+replay_stop_multiplier = 1.20
+allow_replay_threshold_below_foundation_baseline = false
+
+[acceptance]
+maximum_target_force_rmse_ev_per_angstrom = 0.030
+
+[evaluation]
+target_score_weight = 1.0
+replay_score_weight = 1.0
+```
+
+`maximum_replay_force_rmse_ev_per_angstrom` is intentionally derived from the weights and target
+boundary instead of being an independent source of truth.
+
+## Compatibility boundary
+
+Historical DATA8/training-protocol/materialization records without an adaptive-stop policy remain
+readable and retain their original fixed-epoch behavior. New ADAPT-STOP1 protocol identities bind
+the complete adaptive-stop policy, monitor lineage, epoch ceiling, and binary learned-model dtype.
+They must not be silently projected back into pre-ADAPT-STOP1 identities.
